720:   "description": "Will global semiconductor revenue reach roughly $975 billion in calendar 2026, including about $500 billion from AI chip sales, as Deloitte estimates?",
721:   "outcome": "Calendar 2026 global semiconductor industry revenue and the AI-chip portion of it, the aggregate that underpins demand for Nvidia's data center products.",
722:   "period": "Calendar 2026; checked against full-year 2026 semiconductor industry revenue data (e.g., SIA/WSTS annual statistics or Gartner/IDC 2026 revenue releases) published in early 2027. The item gives the 2026 period.",
724:   "settled_by": "SIA/WSTS full-year 2026 global semiconductor sales statistics and Gartner/IDC full-year 2026 semiconductor revenue releases."
730:   "text": "If you'd invested $10,000 in shares of Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) a decade ago, your investment would now be worth almost $1.5 million.\nVarious catalysts have driven the astronomical rise in Nvidia stock over this period. The strong demand for graphics cards used in personal computers (PCs), driven by gaming and cryptocurrency, along with the artificial intelligence (AI)-fueled surge in data center graphics cards, has been instrumental in boosting Nvidia's revenue and earnings in recent years.\nNvidia's robust growth drivers have made it the world's largest company by market cap . Investors, therefore, may be wondering whether this semiconductor bellwether can make them millionaires once again in the long run. Let's see whether Nvidia can replicate its stunning returns over the coming decade and turn $10,000 into a million dollars.\nA 100x jump in Nvidia stock is unlikely, but that's half the story\nNvidia now has a market cap of $5.5 trillion. The stock will need to jump by 100x from current levels to turn $10,000 into a million, which means its market cap will need to exceed $500 trillion for investors to become millionaires.\nThat seems absurd, considering that the size of the global economy is poised to hit $150 trillion in 2030, according to Visual Capitalist. The firm notes that the global gross domestic product (GDP) is on track to grow by $25 trillion between 2026 and 2030. Nvidia, therefore, is unlikely to become larger than the global economy in the long run.\nIn simple words, investing $10,000 in Nvidia right now in the hope that this single investment alone will make you a millionaire is not the right idea. However, buying $10,000 worth of Nvidia's shares as a part of a diversified portfolio could indeed help investors achieve their goal of becoming millionaires over the long run.\nHere's why.\nNvidia can become a much bigger company over the next decade\nNvidia has grown significantly over the last decade. The company's annual revenue in fiscal 2017 (which ended in January 2017) was $6.9 billion. Analysts expect Nvidia's revenue to land at $411 billion in fiscal 2027, an increase of almost 60x in a decade.\nThe good news for Nvidia investors is that it still has a lot of room for growth. Deloitte estimates that the global semiconductor market could be worth $975 billion in 2026, with $500 billion coming from sales of AI chips. Nvidia rival AMD forecasts that sales of AI accelerator chips could hit $1 trillion in 2030. Even better, the overall semiconductor market could be worth $2 trillion in 2036, according to Deloitte, even with moderate growth.\nNvidia is a key player in the global AI chip market with an estimated 80% share. So, the secular growth of the semiconductor market, primarily fueled by AI chips, should ensure healthy long-term growth for Nvidia. Additionally, the emergence of new AI-fueled applications beyond data centers, such as physical AI, and the integration of AI into edge devices, such as PCs, should open additional growth avenues for Nvidia.\nThe physical AI market, for instance, could be worth $430 billion in 2030 and hit $1.6 trillion in 2040, according to a third-party report. Physical AI refers to the integration of AI into real-world objects, such as machines, robots, and vehicles. The integration of this technology in multiple industries, ranging from healthcare to industrial to defense to space to retail, is poised to drive robust growth in this market over the long run.\nNvidia is already strengthening its position in physical AI. The company noted on its recent earnings call that Amazon will adopt its full physical AI stack to automate its warehouse robots. Noetra, a government-backed Japanese company developing physical AI and industrial robotics applications, will also adopt Nvidia's physical AI tools.\nThese growth opportunities indicate why analysts expect Nvidia's revenue to increase at a healthy pace even after the strong base it has already achieved.\nFor comparison, Nvidia reported $215.9 billion in revenue in fiscal 2026 (which ended in January this year). The chart above suggests that its top line is on track to increase 4x in just two years. Even better, analysts have been boosting their long-term earnings growth expectations.\nAssuming Nvidia's earnings indeed increase at an annual pace of 49% for the next five years, its earnings per share will jump to $35 at the end of the forecast period (using fiscal 2026's earnings of $4.77 per share as the base). If Nvidia trades at 21 times earnings at that time, in line with the S&P 500 index's forward earnings multiple, its stock price could jump to $735 in five years.\nThat's nearly 3.2x Nvidia's current stock price, making it an ideal growth stock for investors looking to build a million-dollar portfolio, especially considering that it trades at an attractive 25 times forward earnings even after its terrific growth and sunny prospects.\nNvidia stock has jumped significantly over the past decade, and the good news is that it still has multibagger potential.\nBy Harsh Chauhan – Sep 6, 2026 at 3:23PM EST\nNvidia can become a significantly larger company over the next decade, driven by secular growth in the semiconductor market and new catalysts."
738:   "outcome": "The volume of NAND demand attributable to Nvidia's CMX architecture in calendar 2027, against the item's projection of more than 100 million TB (from 35 million TB in 2026), and whether memory makers' capacity additions keep pace with it.",
747:   "text": "[News] Kioxia Reportedly to Sample CXL Modules Soon, Reaffirms Strong NVIDIA CMX Ties\nAs AI workloads demand ever more memory, DRAM’s physical capacity ceiling is becoming the bottleneck. At a September 3rd briefing, Japan’s NAND giant detailed how its CXL memory modules expand usable memory capacity while cutting latency by over 90% versus conventional approaches, per EE Times Japan .\nAccording to EE Times Japan, Kioxia plans to begin CXL memory module sampling soon.\nCiting Katsuki Matsudera, head of Memory Application Technology at Kioxia’s Memory Division, the report further notes that simulations showed that replacing one-third of DRAM with CXL memory modules would keep performance degradation to less than 5%. Alternatively, replacing part of DRAM with CXL memory modules at the same cost could double capacity while boosting performance by 1.3x, the report adds.\nAs the report notes, AI is driving strong DRAM demand, but DRAM’s limited capacity makes it difficult to scale memory within increasingly data-hungry systems. NAND flash offers far greater capacity, yet comes with its own trade-offs: higher latency and lower write endurance make it unsuitable as a direct replacement for DRAM in main memory.\nAccording to Matsudera, real-world access patterns follow an 80/20 split: roughly 80% of memory accesses hit just 20% of the data, while the rest is accessed far less often. Kioxia’s approach exploits that gap — storing the 80% on flash memory that, like DRAM, can be accessed directly as memory, easing capacity constraints without sacrificing performance.\nBut flash’s inherent latency remained a hurdle. To close that gap, Kioxia developed a dedicated controller with latency-reduction features. Paired with XL-FLASH, the resulting CXL memory module cuts latency by more than 90% compared with conventional approaches, per EE Times Japan.\nBeyond CXL, Kioxia also used the briefing to update its flagship BiCS Flash roadmap. The company began mass production of its 10th-generation BiCS FLASH 3D flash memory at its Kitakami Plant in Iwate starting in July, while also shipping samples of its 1Tb TLC products. Compared with the 8th generation, the new generation delivers a 59% increase in bit density, a 40% improvement in read power efficiency, and a 30% improvement in write power efficiency, per EE Times Japan.\nNotably, BigGo Finance also highlights Kioxia’s reported collaboration with NVIDIA around CMX (Context Memory Storage) — a new storage tier NVIDIA announced in March, built around its BlueField-4 data processing unit to expand GPU memory for long-context conversational and agentic AI inference.\nThat collaboration is already bearing fruit: Kioxia unveiled the CM10 Series in July, its latest enterprise SSDs powered by 10th-generation BiCS FLASH TLC and built to support the NVIDIA CMX architecture, delivering major gains in performance and power efficiency over the previous generation.\nAs Kioxia’s first PCIe 6.0 enterprise SSD, the CM10 Series also supports direct-cold-plate liquid cooling, offering greater flexibility and efficiency for cooling next-generation AI infrastructure, the report adds.\nAccording to the report, Makoto Hamada, General Manager of the SSD Business Division’s Technology Planning Department, noted that the competition is inevitable given the market’s huge potential, but said Kioxia remains closely engaged with NVIDIA and regularly communicates with key executives. “I don’t feel we are falling behind,” he said, adding that Kioxia is also watching emerging storage needs in AI PCs and physical AI, the report suggests.\nAs SeDaily points out, Samsung could be another key player in the market, as the South Korean memory giant is reportedly accelerating its V9 NAND capacity expansion, driven in part by NVIDIA’s next-generation storage architecture, CMX, slated for launch in the second half of the year.\nAccording to SeDaily, NVIDIA’s CMX packs 576 SSDs for 9,600TB of total capacity, while NAND demand for the architecture is projected to surge from 35 million TB this year to more than 100 million TB next year. With Samsung’s NAND output expected to reach around 250 million TB this year, the company is positioning its massive production capacity to capture a larger share of the emerging CMX market, the report adds.\n[News] NVIDIA Vera Rubin Spillover from HBM to NAND: CMX Fuels TLC Spot Price Rebound from June Dip\n[News] Kioxia Steps Up AI NAND Competition With South Korean Rivals, Eyes PCIe 6.0 and UFS 5.0 Mass Production"

